Subject: [PATCH] db: a UNIQUE row key on DeviceList, RadioList, BSSList and STAList, rows written with REPLACE INTO, duplicate rows removed and the key added at load; a statement that would be cut is not run

The tables have no key and every write is a plain INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE; an insert is chosen
without asking the database whenever the entry is not in memory. A write repeated for one row
added a second (DeviceList, rdk-1010, 21 October), and STAList's update matched a station's MAC
and BSSID only while it set the RUID.

A table names the columns that identify a row (set_row_key in its init_columns: ID for DeviceList,
RadioList and BSSList; MACAddress, BSSID, RUID for STAList). create_table adds a UNIQUE key on them;
at load, before the rows are read, a table made without it has its duplicate rows reduced to one
(their values escaped in the WHERE) and the key added, confirmed with SHOW INDEX since the client
cannot tell a failed statement from one without results; a board that has duplicates heals at its
next start. With a key, insert_row writes REPLACE INTO: one statement, one row per key. STAList's
update writes its whole row the same way, so a station's write lands even when the update was
chosen because another row of its MAC existed; its row builder also matches the RUID.

vsnprintf cut a statement silently at the 2048-byte buffer: its length is checked, a statement
that would be cut is logged with its table and not run, and the longest statement per table is
logged as it grows past a quarter of the buffer.

Unit tests: test_l1_db_client.cpp, on scratch tables: planted duplicates removed and the key added
(a value with a quote), REPLACE INTO keeping one row, a cut statement not run, an STA update by its
whole key leaving the station's other rows and writing a missing one.
